---
title: "Licensing: what you build is yours"
description: "Taliesin is AGPL-3.0. The documents you build with it are not: the Output Exception grants you unrestricted rights over your own pages, with nothing to attribute."
---

*Reference: [CLI](cli.tmd) · [Front matter](frontmatter.tmd) · [Cell options](cell-options.tmd) · [Configuration](configuration.tmd) · [Troubleshooting](troubleshooting.tmd) · [Accessibility](accessibility.tmd) · Licensing*

Taliesin is licensed under the **GNU Affero General Public License v3.0**. That governs
*the program*. It makes no claim on the blog posts, lecture notes, papers and books
you write with it.

The licence answers "who owns what I write". The other half of that question — what happens
to your documents if the *program* stops being maintained — is answered separately, with
numbers, in @sec-continuity.

That distinction needs saying out loud, because of how Taliesin builds a page.

## Why the question comes up at all

A built page is self-contained on purpose. There is no CDN, no build step for the reader,
and no network fetch at view time, which is what lets a page work offline and keeps a
document readable in ten years. Taliesin achieves that by copying its own stylesheets and
scripts **into the page it produces** — `base.css`, `tali-js.js`, the `code-enhance/`
fragments, `mermaid.js` for a page with diagrams, and so on.

So a page you build genuinely does contain a copy of some of Taliesin's own AGPL-licensed
source. Read strictly and without any further grant, someone could argue that every page
you publish is therefore an AGPL work, and that publishing it obliges you to offer source
to your readers. That would be an absurd reading of what the licence is for, but "absurd"
is not the same as "answered".

## The answer

It is answered, in [`LICENSE-OUTPUT-EXCEPTION.md`](https://github.com/AJBogo9/taliesin/blob/main/LICENSE-OUTPUT-EXCEPTION.md)
at the repository root: an **additional permission under AGPL section 7**, the same
instrument GCC uses so that compiling a program with GCC does not make the program GPL.

In short:

- **Your document is yours.** Building or serving it with Taliesin does not make it, its
  source, or its contents an AGPL work.
- **Nothing to attribute.** You do not have to reproduce the exception, the AGPL, a
  copyright line, or an offer of source anywhere in a page you build.
- **Publishing is not a section 13 trigger.** Hosting output that contains Taliesin's
  runtime does not, by itself, oblige you to offer anyone source.
- **It applies however you produced the output** — `build` or `preview`.

You may publish, sell, host or relicense what you build, under any terms you choose.

## What the AGPL still covers

Taliesin itself. If you modify Taliesin and run *that* as a network service, you owe its
users the corresponding source — which is the whole point of choosing the AGPL, and the
part the exception deliberately leaves intact. The runtime assets are AGPL **as source in
the repository**; the exception speaks only to the copies that land in a built document.

Vendored third-party assets (KaTeX, D3, Observable Plot, Mermaid) keep their own licences,
all permissive, all imposing no copyleft obligation on your document. They are listed in
`THIRD_PARTY.md` and `crates/core/assets/js/LICENSES.md`.

::: {.callout-note}
## The one-sentence version

If you only wanted to know whether you can publish what you wrote: **yes**, under any terms
you like, with nothing to attribute.
:::
